        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Marignano</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">13–14 September 1515</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the town now called Melegnano, 16 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on a plain of vineyards, fields, orchards and pastures near San Giuliano</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a>, with German landsknechts and Venetian allies under Bartolomeo d'Alviano who arrived in the morning of the second day; the Swiss, about 22,000 men, almost all pike infantry with fewer than a dozen guns. The Swiss were heavily outnumbered and outgunned.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A French victory: the Swiss withdrew in good order, the French regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and the Treaty of Fribourg of 1516 ended Swiss claims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of Milan.</span></div>
