    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1513</span><span class="rf-text">The Swiss win at Novara, take control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and restore Maximilian Sforza as duke</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1515</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a> leads his army over the Col d'Argentière, and the French capture the papal commander Prospero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> at Villafranca</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1515</span><span class="rf-text">Swiss columns of about 22,000 leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and meet the French at Marignano, where the fighting lasts into the night</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1515</span><span class="rf-text">The Swiss attack again in the morning, and the arrival of the Venetians turns the battle against them</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1515</span><span class="rf-text">Francis meets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, and the two agree the terms of a concordat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1516</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of Fribourg, the Perpetual Peace, is signed between Francis and the Swiss cantons</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Swiss Confederacy conclude a service pact that makes Swiss regiments a regular part of the French armed forces</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-marignano" data-v="5ae6dcf52e" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The French form up and the Swiss approach</b> The French, who had not expected battle, formed in three divisions. The vanguard held two shallow rises with crossbowmen, a Landsknecht square and arquebusiers, the king's force stood two kilometres behind, and the Swiss came on in three divisions.</li><li><b>The Swiss forlorn hope charges the guns</b> A forlorn hope detached from the Swiss vanguard and charged the grand battery with lowered pikes. It drove back the Landsknecht defenders and captured a few of the guns, the speed of its advance making the French fire ineffective.</li><li><b>Bourbon's cavalry strikes the flank</b> Cavalry from the French right counter-attacked the forlorn hope's flank and drove it back to the shelter of the Swiss vanguard.</li><li><b>Night fighting by moonlight</b> Smoke and darkness hid the fighting. French cavalry charges, often led by the king, threw back temporary Swiss gains, and the pursuing horse of the first counter-attack fell back a full kilometre after heavy losses.</li><li><b>The Swiss charge the guns again at dawn</b> The Swiss reformed their largest phalanx and charged the reassembled battery. Point-blank cannon fire slowed it and stopped it pushing farther, though the Landsknechts were again driven back, and a cavalry charge led by Bayard forced the Swiss to give ground.</li><li><b>The Swiss turn on the French left</b> Baffled by the artillery, the Swiss turned their assault on Alençon's left-wing division. They made some headway, and the attack was then thrown back.</li><li><b>The Venetians arrive and the Swiss withdraw</b> The Venetian forces under d'Alviano arrived and turned the tide. With their attacks repulsed everywhere, the Swiss gave ground and withdrew in a single large open square.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza.jpg" alt="Ludovico Sforza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pala_Sforzesca_-_detail_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Master, Italian (active 1490-1520 in Lombardy), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The campaign of Marignano followed years of Swiss successes, during which French fortunes in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had suffered greatly. The Swiss had taken control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> after their victory at the Battle of Novara in 1513 and returned Maximilian Sforza, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a>, to the ducal throne, making Milan a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Milan was the gateway to Italy.</p><p>The prologue to the battle was an Alpine passage, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a>, the newly crowned King of France, hauled 72 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> over new-made roads across the Col d'Argentière, a previously unknown route. At Villafranca the French, led by Jacques de la Palice, captured the papal commander Prospero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> in a cavalry raid deep behind the allied lines, with the Chevalier Bayard providing the impetus and expertise. They also took a great deal of booty, including 600 horses.</p><p>The capture of Colonna and the sudden appearance of the French army on the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> stunned the allies. The Pope and the Swiss both sought terms with Francis, while the Spanish allies on their way from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> halted to await developments. The main Swiss army retreated to Milan, and a large faction, tired of the war and eager to go home with the booty of years of campaigning, urged terms with the French.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon.jpg" alt="Charles III, Duke of Bourbon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon">Charles III, Duke of Bourbon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connetable_Bourbon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean Clouet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A peace agreement that gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> back to the French led large parts of the Swiss army to go home, but fresh troops arrived from cantons that refused to abide by the treaty. Discord swept through the Swiss forces until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthaus-schiner">Matthäus Schiner</a>, cardinal of Sion and an enemy of Francis, tricked them into battle against a far superior force by exaggerating the extent of a minor skirmish. The Swiss left Milan in disciplined columns, numbering around 22,000. They consisted almost entirely of pike infantry, fielded fewer than a dozen artillery pieces and had no cavalry.</p><p>The French were not expecting battle, since a treaty had been signed. Francis was in his tent, trying on a new suit of armour, when scouts reported the Swiss. The army formed in three divisions. The vanguard, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon">Charles III, Duke of Bourbon</a>, and Marshal Gian Giacomo Trivulzio, held two shallow rises near San Giuliano, with crossbowmen behind earthworks and pavise shields on the left, a large square of landsknecht infantry in the centre and arquebusiers on the right, covering the artillery, which stood in a crescent along the south-eastern and eastern edges.</p><p>Two kilometres behind, Francis led the cavalry and 6,000 landsknechts recruited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, known as the Black Band of Gueldern. A kilometre further back the rearguard was led by the king's brother-in-law Charles IV, Duke of Alençon. The French had a good mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, arquebusiers and cavalry. The Swiss, trusting to experience that a rapid advance would sweep all before them, approached in three dense masses of pikemen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Close to sunset the Swiss advanced, and a forlorn hope detached from their vanguard charged the grand battery in front of the king's position with lowered pikes, intending to overrun the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and turn them on their owners. At first the attack drove back the landsknecht defenders and took a few guns, the speed of the advance making the French fire ineffective. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>'s cavalry then counter-attacked from the French right and drove the forlorn hope back to the shelter of the Swiss vanguard. The pursuing French horse took heavy losses and fell back a full kilometre.</p><p>Smoke and night hid the fighting, and in the moonlight and confusion the outcome hung in the balance. French cavalry charges, often led by the king himself with Bayard at his side, threw back temporary Swiss gains time and again. Many leading French commanders were wounded or killed, among them the Prince of Tallemont, son of Louis II de la Trémoille, who died with sixty-two wounds. Bayard cut his way through the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> to rescue the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. In the darkest hours both armies stopped and reorganised, and some Swiss soldiers, even whole units, slipped away.</p><p>The battle began again in the morning. The grand battery had been reassembled, and the Swiss reformed their largest phalanx and again charged the guns. Massed cannon fire at point-blank range stopped them, though they closed up and pushed on, and a cavalry charge led by Bayard forced them to give ground. They turned against Alençon's division on the left and were thrown back there too. Only the arrival in mid-morning of the Venetians under Bartolomeo d'Alviano turned the tide. The Swiss gave ground and withdrew, forming a single gigantic open square and keeping extraordinary discipline.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x.jpg" alt="Pope Leo X" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raffaello,_ritratto_di_papa_leone_X_tra_i_cardinali_luigi_de'_rossi_e_giulio_de'_medici,_1518,_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francesco Bini, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The retreating Swiss army kept control of Bellinzona and the adjacent valleys of upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, leaving a rearguard to hold them. Francis gained the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and, more importantly, the Castello Sforzesco within it, the strategic key to control of Lombardy. Maximilian Sforza, the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and the cardinal-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Sion had retreated there, and submitted only when French sappers had placed mines under the foundations. Maximilian went into exile with a French purse of 100,000 écus.</p><p>After lengthy negotiations a peace was signed at Fribourg on 29 November 1516 between the Thirteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Cantons</a> and their allies and Francis, as both King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Duke of Milan. In this Treaty of Fribourg, the Perpetual Peace, the Swiss Confederacy gave up all claims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of Milan, and France paid 700,000 gold crowns in compensation. Each side agreed not to support the other's enemies, apart from existing treaties, and to arbitrate future disputes. France offered another 300,000 crowns if the Swiss would yield their transmontane territories, except Bellinzona, but this was declined. The treaty gave the Swiss trade privileges in Milan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, and only the Ossola valley went back to Milan.</p><p>On 11 December 1515 Francis met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, where it was agreed that he would renounce the Pragmatic Sanction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a> and the pope would accept his demands on the choice of French bishops. The concordat was signed by Leo X on 15 August 1516. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>, also at Bologna, was persuaded to go to France, where Francis gave him the Clos Lucé manor and a pension of 7,000 scudi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known partly through the words of those who took part. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a> reported to his mother that thirty brave charges had been hurled by the French gendarmerie against the stubborn Swiss, a boast that shows how he wished the fighting to be seen. He also ordered the medal that claimed a victory over those whom only Caesar had vanquished. Marshal Trivulzio, a veteran of every war for the previous 40 years, called it the battle of giants and said that all earlier battles of his lifetime had been child's sport.</p><p>How to weigh the victory is disputed. It could be called the expected result, since the Swiss were heavily outnumbered and outgunned, but the Swiss had often won from such positions and the French victory did not come easily. The immediate causes of the Swiss defeat are given as their failure to take the French artillery early and the arrival of the Venetians, with a weak command structure behind them: earlier in 1515 the Swiss diet had let each captain decide whether his company would go on or retreat. Swiss historiography has also tended to credit a lesson learned at Marignano with the end of Swiss offensive wars, though the later division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and defeats at Bicocca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesia">Sesia</a> and Pavia were at least as effective.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle ended all Swiss aspirations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. The Swiss Confederacy never again fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or Milan, did not go to war against anyone after 1525, and apart from Bern's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> in 1536, acting on its own, launched no further offensive against an external enemy. The Perpetual Peace was kept for the rest of the lifetime of the Kingdom of France and was broken only when the French Republic invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1798. It opened three centuries of close ties between the Confederacy and France, while Switzerland moved away from its association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. The service pact of 1521 made Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> regiments a regular part of the French armed forces. The arrangement outlasted three centuries, with four Swiss regiments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, until foreign military service of Swiss citizens was outlawed in 1848.</p><p>French success at Marignano eventually galvanised opposition in the divided peninsula and turned the European balance of power against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, present at the battle as church patron at Glarus, blamed Cardinal Schiner's warmongering for the disaster, became an outspoken opponent of mercenary service and began to preach against the high clergy, the first signs of the radicalisation that led to the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><p>The battle is commemorated by Pierre Bontemps's bas-relief on Francis I's tomb at Saint-Denis, a painting by Antoine Caron for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, now in the National Gallery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and Clément Janequin's chanson La guerre. The Swiss hero Hans Bär appears in a fresco on the tower of the Basel City Hall, and Jean Daetwyler's march Marignan of 1939 is the official orchestral hymn of the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Marignano on 13 and 14 September 1515 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a>'s French army, with landsknechts and Venetian allies, beat back a Swiss force of about 22,000 that had charged its guns. The Swiss gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in the Perpetual Peace of 1516, and close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> followed.</em></p>

